WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:06.172
NARRATOR: South Africa's Western
Cape is notoriously dangerous.

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:10.310
Almost one in four of all
fatal great white attacks

00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:12.312
happen here.

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:15.181
In other parts of the world,
the most dangerous time

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:19.018
to enter the water is at
dawn or dusk, the times when

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.821
white sharks typically hunt.

00:00:21.888 --> 00:00:24.457
But these waters are different.

00:00:24.524 --> 00:00:27.060
Investigators have
identified that the majority

00:00:27.127 --> 00:00:31.464
of the attacks on the Western
Cape take place during the day.

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:35.201
This surprising anomaly
could be the key to solving

00:00:35.268 --> 00:00:36.903
the mystery behind the attacks.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:43.843
Mike Barron is a marine
biologist living in Cape Town.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.146
For the past two
years, he's been

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:50.950
studying the oceanic
ecosystems of the Western Cape.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:53.520
Mike believes part
of the answer lies

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:57.657
in an incredible aspect of
South Africa's marine ecology.

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:59.225
MIKE BARRON: So today,
we're going to drop down

00:00:59.292 --> 00:01:01.694
and I'm going to monitor one
of my favorite places to dive.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:03.830
It's a really amazing,
magical place.

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:14.707
[music playing]

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:17.143
NARRATOR: These are South
Africa's kelp forests.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.583
Each strand of
this giant seaweed

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.354
measures up to 40 feet tall
and grows in dense clusters

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:32.091
stretching over
600 miles, Creating

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:35.695
a vast, underwater wonderland.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:37.497
MIKE BARRON: All the
different complexities

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:39.566
of life down there,
and it's a fantastic

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.836
area to dive and research.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:44.838
NARRATOR: The kelp
forests are home

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:48.608
to all manner of oceanic life.

00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:51.010
But there is one
species that lives here

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:53.513
of particular
significance when it comes

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.282
to sharks, cape fur seals.

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:00.687
MIKE BARRON: White
sharks are often

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.989
seen patrolling the
edges of kelp forests

00:02:03.056 --> 00:02:05.692
looking for the cape fur seal.

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:08.061
NARRATOR: Until recently, it
was believed that great whites

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:12.298
patrolled the edges of the
kelp forests at dawn and dusk,

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:14.567
but rarely ventured in.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:17.036
The sun's low
position in the sky

00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:18.538
was thought to
help them pick out

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:21.107
the silhouettes of
unsuspecting seals

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:24.043
as they exited the forest.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:28.314
But in 2019, a study
emerged that turned

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:31.618
traditional thinking about
great whites' hunting patterns

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:33.987
on its head.

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.322
MIKE BARRON: In 2019,
researchers were conducting

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.057
a study on great white sharks.

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:42.729
They attached some cameras
to the dorsal fins,

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:47.066
and the footage that came
back was very unusual.

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:49.936
That actually found that these
sharks were moving into quite

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:53.940
dense forest areas,
and even hunting

00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:56.776
seals, which
previously we thought

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.278
was not happening at all.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.580
NARRATOR: And most
importantly, the sharks

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:03.349
were seen to be
hunting during the day,

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.618
not just at dawn and dusk.

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:08.621
MIKE BARRON: This new
revelation in shark research

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:11.124
means that all the way through
today, wherever there's kelp,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.059
there's potentially
white sharks cruising

00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:14.294
around, looking for their seal.

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:19.332
NARRATOR: South Africa
is one of a few countries

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:21.668
in the world that
has natural kelp

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:25.471
forests right on its coast.

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:27.340
MIKE BARRON: Kelp grows
all along the beaches

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:29.943
here in the Western Cape,
right from the intertidal zone

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:33.813
and very highly populated
beaches as well.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.348
There's lots of people
swimming, surfing,

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:38.484
and diving in the forests
right along the coast.

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:42.221
NARRATOR: This means that every
day at any time, the sharks

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:44.958
are patrolling the coastline
of the Western Cape,

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:50.897
looking for seals, in the exact
same waters used by people.

